A||||26||Formula88||Smokey & The Bandit||||10||
Z||000000||Formula88||07-03-2001||09:13 AM||||So I was reading some of the posts about the Bandit Trans Am and wanted to add a little.  Yeah, the late 70's T/A's were pretty sad, in comparison to what they used to be (or have since become).  The 455 in 76 (last year of the 455) only had 200HP.  Sad.  But, there WAS a special edition "Bandit" Trans Am sold by Pontiac.  Done by an outside company, similar to the Firehawks are today.  I don't remember the company, but the car was available in 1980, had a 455, and a Doug Nash 5-speed.  It was emissions legal and could run 0-60 in about 5 sec.  It also pulled about .83g in the corners (incredible for the time).  This was from a Motor Trend magazine back then.  Unfortunately I don't remember the month, but if someone were interested enough, I'm sure the article can be found.<P>Now, my favorite 2nd Generation T/A would have to be my old '72.  455HO, round port heads, aluminum 2pc intake manifold and an 800cfm Quadrabog from the factory.  Yum Yum!  Gotta love big blocks!  It only had 8.4:1 compression (non-HO's had 8.2 and small D-port heads).  Totally different philosophy on a car, though.  My Fiero slices corners like a scalpel.  The T/A just straightenend them out like a steam roller.  <P>Oh, and for the late-70's T/A spotters out there, check the shaker sticker.  If it says "6.6 Litre" its an Olds 403 engine with 180HP and an automatic.  If it says "T/A 6.6" it's the Pontiac 400, with 220HP and was available with a Muncie 4-speed.  Of course, that's assuming the decals are accurate for the car. I wouldn't mind getting a '79 T/A 6.6 4-speed.  That was the last year of the Pontiac 400.  They actually stopped building the 400s in 78, but since they knew 79 was the T/A's 10th Anniversary, the built some extras and stashed them for use in the Anniversary models and regualar T/A's till they ran out.  Makes the '79 T/A 6.6's kinda unique and a bit rare.  <P>Ok, I'm done now.  We now return you to your regularly scheduled chat.||||reg||10
Z||000001||LZeitgeist||07-03-2001||09:27 AM||||Oh, yeah? I'll race your old '72 with my stock Fiero Formula any day of the week. And win.<P>Come get some.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000002||Oreif||07-03-2001||09:43 AM||||Nunzio out of New York built the "Bandit" Trans Am's of the mid/late 70's. The 1977 "Bandit Special" had a Pontiac 400 engine that had 300hp. There were a total of 350 "Bandit Specials" From 1977 thru 1983. <BR>77 thru 1979 used Pontiac 400's,<BR>1980 and 1981 used Turbo/intercooled 301's with 235hp.<BR>1982 and 1983 had Chevy 305's with 240hp.<BR>(Source: High Performance Pontiac Magazine)||||reg||2
Z||000003||Slammed Fiero||07-03-2001||09:44 AM||||I would like to know where you got your info on the trans amn , We had a smokey and the bandit TA , rated at 180hp it was a dog , and it was fully optioned. I remeber seeing a few run at norwalk to the tune of high 15's in the 1/4. IF you know the aproximate date of the magazine , I can get a copy of it.<P>JM ||||reg||1
Z||000004||Songman||07-03-2001||10:45 AM||||I gotta agree with Oreif on this.. The Bandit was a lot more than just a black T/A with gold stripes. I have a buddy that has a 77 and that is one bad @ss car! Not every black and gold T/A was a bandit. You gotta remember when we are talking about too. No car was very hot then. Think about the 75-77 Corvette. They were dogs! The Bandit was a rare breed in those days.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/songman_1.gif"> <BR><A HREF="http://members.home.com/tnfieros" TARGET=_blank>Tennessee Fiero Owners</A>||||reg||1
Z||000005||Formula88||07-03-2001||11:30 AM||||The Bandit T/A I'm referring to came out in 80 or 81, and yes, it had a 455.  It wasn't done by Nunzi Romano.  I would have remembered his name being associated with it.  He may very well have done the "Bandit Specials" Orief referred to.  These were different years.  Yeah, there is a big misconception about what makes a "Bandit" T/A.  Most people call any black Trans Am, The Bandit edition, which couldn't be farther from the truth.  I'm unfamiliar with the one's Orief mentioned, but would love to find out more.  Especially what they did to get the 82-83 305 up to 240HP.  It had 145HP with a 4-V, 165HP with Crossfire Injection, and the 83 L69 305 HO had 190HP.  They must have done some righteous mods to get it 95HP above the base LG4 305.<P>Oh, and the 80/81 Turbo 301's didn't use an intercooler, unless that was part of the Bandit package.  That could have easily bumped it up to 235HP. <P>Slammed, sounds like you just had a run of the mill 403 Olds engined T/A.  Yeah, they were dogs.  I'll be the shaker said 6.6 Litre, didn't it?   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> <p>[This message has been edited by Formula88 (edited 07-03-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000006||Formula88||07-03-2001||11:35 AM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by LZeitgeist:<BR><B>Oh, yeah? I'll race your old '72 with my stock Fiero Formula any day of the week. And win.<P>Come get some.    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>If I still had the car, I'd be happy to oblige.  And while I'm sure the Formula handles better, I think you'd be surprised at how well that tank handled.  Especially for the year.  According to Car & Driver, in 71, the TA pulled .83g on F60-15 bias ply tires!  <P>As for the straight line?  Well, you know the Fiero <B>will</B> fit down the secondary venturi's of that Quadrajet.  Don't start sounding like Steve did with his Motley Carlo SS.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">  I sure did love blowing his doors off repeatedly.  Wasn't a challenge, but the fact that he always thought if he tried a little harder, he'd have a chance.  Never mind the 120HP and 250lb-ft of Torque disadvantage he had.  It's a shame we didn't hang out when I had that car.  I sold it right around the time I met you and Larry and the rest of the crew.  Larry never rode in it, but he remembers it.  Steve and Taz helped me put the engine in.  That was a bigger job than the clutch in my Fiero!  But it was done in the same car port!  So the circle of Horsepower continues....   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><p>[This message has been edited by Formula88 (edited 07-03-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000007||LZeitgeist||07-03-2001||11:44 AM||||Nah... you'd probably whup my butt with it when you had it back *then*... I mean now... you know, now that it's sitting in that guys yard with weeds growing up around it.<P>I mean heck - I'm not *that* easy...  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000008||ray b||07-03-2001||01:25 PM||||the HO 's were the best 455 heads ever<BR>very hard to find and very $$$$ if you did<BR>i found a 71 4 bolt HO block for my 68 GTO<BR>it came to me with ramair #3 heads at 13/1<BR>but never could find the HO heads, sd or ramair#4 were much more common round ports heads.<BR>trans am's after the sd were very slow stock<BR>turbo were a bomb[boom kind]way over priced tooo 35k in 80????<P>------------------<BR>Question wonder and be wierd||||reg||1
Z||000009||Formula88||07-03-2001||02:15 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by ray b:<BR><B>trans am's after the sd were very slow stock<BR>turbo were a bomb[boom kind]way over priced tooo 35k in 80????<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Do what?  They stickered around $12000 new.  I remember seeing them on the lot.  Even a new Vette didn't go for that much. Now, the Bandit TA I was talking about with the 455 in 80 or 81, I believe that was listed at $30000, complete.  But that also included an aftermarket warranty to cover everything that was voided in the factory warranty.||||reg||11
Z||000010||Formula88||07-03-2001||02:36 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by LZeitgeist:<BR><B>Nah... you'd probably whup my butt with it when you had it back *then*... I mean now... you know, now that it's sitting in that guys yard with weeds growing up around it.<P>I mean heck - I'm not *that* easy...   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Sorry, wrong car.  The one rotting out in Knightdale was my old '69 Firebird 350.  My first car ever.  The TA was sold to an enthusiast.  He came over, checked the codes to make sure it was all there - started the engine and said "I'll take it."  He knew what he was getting.  I hope the TA is fairing better than my '69 did.  And if it wasn't for the differential damage, I'd have bought the '69 back.<P>The Firebird would be a good match for our Fieros.  It was a 2V (well, I put a 4V on it), so it was only rated at 250bhp.  So that's probably about 200net HP, and with the extra weight, and an automatic, the Fiero could be competitive.  Now, the TA, on the other hand was rated at 300net HP, and that was with a throttle stop that kept the secondaries on the Quarabog from opening all the way (for insurance purposes).  Yup, that was the first thing I did.  It still had the throttle stop when I bought it!  Those little pieces of plastic are probably rare collectibles now.  <P>Oh, and if anyone cares, 72 was also the last year of the functional shaker hood scoop.  Mine was solenoid actuated to open the scoop under full throttle.  And the factory ratchet shifter sure made for one fun automobile, even if it was an automatic.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000011||qwikgta||07-03-2001||11:48 PM||||Mecham or something like that, it was a couple of brothers who did it.  They popped up again not to long ago with another version on the gen 4 body.  95-96 I think.  <P>ROb<P>------------------<BR>qwikgta@yahoo.com<BR>88 T-Top coupe, 65K, Header no Cat, 16" GTZ rims, short shifter, swaybar, open element air cleaner, hood vents.<BR>http://robsfieroproject.homestead.com/index.html<BR>http://vafiero.com<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sigpic_1.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000012||Oreif||07-04-2001||01:46 AM||||Formula88, The one your talking about with the 455, Was that the ones done by the guy who stuffed a 454 chevy into an 84 T/A and screamed across the salt flats and broke some record with it? I can't think of the guys name. I know he did some special editions before his salt flat blast. I think he's the guy who did the "Red Bird" Formula in 1979 with the Overhead cam pontiac 400 engine. I remember it was chain drive and it ran like a rocket but the reliability of the engine was very poor. <BR>As for the Nunzio "Bandit" cars, They were all built for speed. If I remember correctly all the dash panels of the original Bandits had Burt Renoylds signature on them. Somewhere in one of my many boxes buried in my parents basement is the Nunzio story and it tells what he did to the cars. I'll see if I can dig it up this weekend.||||reg||1
Z||000013||LZeitgeist||07-04-2001||12:25 PM||||Cool - I've always been interested in the 'Bandit' cars as well... I had no idea there were so many versions floating around.||||reg||1
Z||000014||Formula88||07-04-2001||01:43 PM||||Orief, could have been. Not sure.  I'll see if I can find the old Motor Trend.  It was around 80 or 81, so that may be kinda difficult.  Maybe I can find a reprint.  I almost want to say it was a company similar to Cars and Concepts.  Not a one man operation like Nunzi.  (BTW, it's Nunzi, not Nunzio  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">)  I've been a fan of The Nunze since his days as Tech Editor for Thunder Am magazine - which has since become High Performance Pontiac.  I believe the ones you're referring to were the officially liscensed ones, as I do remember them having Burt Reynold's sig on them.   If you can find those old mags, that would be great.  I'll see if I can track down the old TA and MT issues.<P>I never cared for the Mecham "Macho" conversions.  I always thought they were too gaudy - and that was back in the '70s disco era!  Oooh, didn't think that was possible back then.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>BTW, Nunzi is still running his shop in Brooklyn NY.  He was recently in HPP with his son racing one of his 455SD Trans Ams in a "Street Shootout" article.  ||||reg||6
