Z||000017||Formula88||07-05-2001||12:02 PM||||If you go back and watch the origianl Smokey and the Bandit, they never say, but allude to the fact that the car is highly modified.  When asking for money, Bandit says,"I'll need a car"<BR>...midget counting out money...<BR>"A speedy car."<BR>...midget counting out money...<BR>"Speedier than that."<BR>...midget hands him wad o cash.<P>Even so, in 77-78, it would have been a 400 4-speed with 200-220 HP.  Strong for it's day, but nothing special.  And judging by it's sound, it was no where near stock.<P>And yeah, the 89 turbo TA's were about $30K, plus dealer markup.  The 80-81's were about $12-14K.  I still wouldn't mind getting an 81 Turbo TA.  Only American production car with a turbo V8.  Of course, I'd dump the turbo 301 and put in a 455....but that's me.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||6
Z||000018||LZeitgeist||07-05-2001||12:51 PM||||Hey, will you help me put the turbo V8 into my car for, um... testing purposes?  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000019||rogergarrison||07-05-2001||01:26 PM||||I had a 72 TA 455 HD or SD (i forget which it was) Lots faster than HO. It would burn the tires off when it kicked down at 90 mph. (auto) Only got like 7 mpg on premium. The turbo TA's were definatly dogs as in St Bernards. The best handling ones in my opinion, were always the formulas, not the TA's.||||reg||1
Z||000020||Formula88||07-05-2001||10:48 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by rogergarrison:<BR><B>I had a 72 TA 455 HD or SD (i forget which it was) Lots faster than HO. It would burn the tires off when it kicked down at 90 mph. (auto) Only got like 7 mpg on premium. The turbo TA's were definatly dogs as in St Bernards. The best handling ones in my opinion, were always the formulas, not the TA's.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>In 71-72, it was a 455H.O.  In 73-74, you could get both a standard 455 and the 455S.D.  There never was a 455HD.  And to burn the tires at 90, it must have had some interesting gearing.  My 72 would be at the top of 2nd gear at 90.  Might could squeeze 100 or so out of 2nd.  At any rate, any car that can break traction at 90 isn't stock, not if it's an automatic.  <BR>But the 7mpg sounds right.  My 455HO got 8mpg no matter what.  But with only 8.4:1 compression, it didn't need anything higher than regular.  If yours required premium, I'd say it was a high compression rebuild.  Throw in a few other racing oriented parts and you could easily have a car that'll break traction at 90.  <P>The Formulas and Trans Ams always have had the same suspension.  The only difference was the body style.  Just like the Formula Fiero and GT.  The Formula Firebird might be a bit lighter, so that could help.  Especially in 70-72 when the Formulas used a fiberglass hood and the TA used steel.  And the Formula had a 350 standard, but could be optioned all the way up to the 455HO or SD depending on year.  A 73-74 SD455 Formula Firebird is one of the rarest Firebirds ever made!  And came with the TA shaker hood instead of the front facing Formula hood scoops.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000021||Philphine||07-05-2001||10:56 PM||||don't know anything about it but there's a '70s black and gold trans am for sale about a mile from my house. looks good if nothing else. i can stop and check the for sale sign for a number if someones interested.||||reg||1
Z||000022||Songman||07-05-2001||11:02 PM||||Check it out... would be interested to see. I want another T/A. The last one I had was a 79 with a 403 Olds... <P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/songman_1.gif"> <BR><A HREF="http://members.home.com/tnfieros" TARGET=_blank>Tennessee Fiero Owners</A>||||reg||1
Z||000023||BV MotorSports||07-05-2001||11:12 PM||||Watching speedvisions American Muscle Car show they claim the 74 455SD was the fastest of the 1-2nd gen Firebird and could run mid 13's off the show room floor. Said it was the king of the hill for 'birds (production) till the 89 3.8 turbo t/a came out.<P>Always wanted one. I know where a 81 Turbo T/A is setting...in a j-yard. It hasnt been picked for parts and the owner says its for sale complete with title. Its been neglected for a long time. I'd take it since he says its numbers matching but its too far gone for me. Its cheaper to by a nice one than to fix a "project".<BR>If anyone has to have it, shoot me an e-mail and I will get his contact info.<P>Steven<P>------------------<BR>87 Fiero GT 14.9/ 89mph<BR>94 Formula WS6 w/ NOS 12.4 /118mph<BR>87 Conquest TSi <BR>87 Conquest TSi 406 SBC V8!<BR>85.5 Starion ESi||||reg||1
Z||000024||AusFiero||07-06-2001||12:15 AM||||There is a Bandit for sale in Aus at the moment. Not just a black and gold one but a proper one. Was imported new by a rich property developer in the early 80s and converted to RHD. He died not long after and it sat in a wharehouse until now,  as his also property development son is in trouble with the tax man and has ordered lots of their stuff to be sold.<P>It is a smick car I have been told too, immaculantly maintained and has only 3500 original KMs on the clock. Is expected to fetch AU$25,000 or about US$13,000<P>Hmmm come to think of it we do have a nice selection of low mileage USA cars here. Lots of people only buy them for Sunday drives.<P>------------------<BR>    <IMG SRC="http://www.jadeweb.com.au/fiero/fiero5.gif">    <BR><A HREF="http://www.jadeweb.com.au/fiero" TARGET=_blank>My Fiero</A><BR>Need a Subwoofer Console or Speaker Door Pods?<p>[This message has been edited by AusFiero (edited 07-06-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000025||AusFiero||07-06-2001||12:22 AM||||And a bit of nostalgica.  A picture of the inside of Burts Bandit.<P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/81ta007.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000026||rogergarrison||07-06-2001||01:19 PM||||No it wasnt stock, It was a Michigan Dealerships sponsored drag car. But it was completely street legal. The wholesale dealer I worked for at the time got it with a load of trade ins from them. I bought it rite off our transport truck. <P>I cant verify it, but Im pretty sure that formulas had different suspension parts. It may been coincidence, but all the formulas I drove would outcorner TA/s all the time.<p>[This message has been edited by rogergarrison (edited 07-06-2001).]||||reg||1
